<DocsLayout
  slug="github-app"
  title="Connecting your repository"
  description="Install the GitHub App, pick a branch, and you're done — for good."
>
  <h2 id="installing-the-app">Installing the GitHub App</h2>
  <p>
    From the dashboard, "Connect GitHub" starts GitHub's own App installation flow. You choose
    which repositories pkgr.app can access — all of them, or a hand-picked selection — directly on
    GitHub's side, not inside pkgr.app.
  </p>

  <h2 id="one-branch-per-project">One branch per project</h2>
  <p>
    Each project tracks exactly one repository and branch. Want builds from more than one branch?
    Create a second project pointing at the same repo — each keeps its own secrets and history
    independently.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-this-unlocks">What this unlocks</h2>
  <p>
    Once connected, pushes to the configured branch can start builds automatically, and you can
    trigger builds manually at any time — see <a href="/docs/builds"
      >triggering &amp; managing builds</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="revoking-access">Revoking access</h2>
  <p>
    Revoke access anytime from GitHub's own "Installed GitHub Apps" settings. pkgr.app immediately
    loses the ability to read the repository or trigger workflows. Your existing build history and
    artifacts stay put until you delete the project yourself.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
